.TH SED 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
sed \- stream editor for filtering and transforming text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sed
.RI [ options ]
.I script
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B sed
.RI [ options ]
.BI "\-e " script
\&...\&
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B sed
.RI [ options ]
.BI "\-f " scriptfile
\&...\&
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sed
reads text from each
.IR file ,
applies a script of editing commands to it a line at a time, and writes
the result to standard output.
With
.BR \-i ,
it instead replaces each
.I file
with the result.
This is the busybox
.BR sed .
It has most of the POSIX
.B sed
and some GNU extensions, but not all of them; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
.B sed
reads standard input.
The files are read as one stream: line numbers keep counting from one
file to the next, and
.B $
is the last line of the last file.
With
.BR \-i ,
each file is a stream of its own.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, the
other files are still read, and the exit status is 1.
.PP
The script comes from the
.B \-e
and
.B \-f
options.
When there are none, it is the first operand.
The scripts of several options make one script, as if joined by
newlines, with every
.B \-e
script before any
.B \-f
file, whatever their order on the command line.
A line of script that ends in a backslash continues on the next line or
in the next option, joined to it by a newline in place of the backslash.
.PP
For each input line,
.B sed
copies the line, without its terminator, into the
.IR "pattern space" ,
runs the commands of the script on it in order, and then writes the
pattern space and the terminator, unless
.B \-n
was given or a command deleted it.
A second buffer, the
.IR "hold space" ,
starts out empty and keeps its contents from line to line.
Some commands read further lines into the pattern space during the
cycle.
.PP
A line ends at an LF or at a NUL byte, and may be any length.
A CR directly before the LF is removed, so CRLF text is processed as if
it had LF line endings, unless
.B \-b
is given.
The automatic write, the
.B p
flag of
.BR s ,
and
.B w
end the line with the terminator it was read with, or with an LF after
.B G
or
.BR x .
A last line that had no terminator is written without one, and an LF is
added before any later output.
Text written by
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
.BR c ,
.BR r ,
.BR = ,
.BR p ,
and
.B P
always ends with an LF.
.SS Script syntax
Commands are separated by newlines or semicolons, and blanks, newlines,
and extra semicolons before a command are skipped.
The separator may be left out after
.B y
and after a command that takes no argument, as in
.BR pd .
A command is an optional address or range, an optional
.BR ! ,
and a command letter with its arguments, with blanks allowed before the
command letter and around the
.BR ! .
A script given with
.B \-e
or as an operand may contain newlines.
.PP
The commands
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
.BR c ,
.BR r ,
and
.BR w ,
and the
.B w
flag of
.BR s ,
take the rest of the line, so no semicolon or
.B }
can follow them on it.
A label, for the commands
.BR : ,
.BR b ,
.BR t ,
and
.BR T ,
ends at a blank, newline, or semicolon, so it includes a
.B }
that follows it directly.
.PP
A
.B #
where a command could start begins a comment that runs to the end of
the line.
A comment whose text starts with
.BR n ,
such as
.B #n
or
.BR #note ,
acts like
.BR \-n ,
wherever in the script it appears.
.SS Addresses
A command may be preceded by one address, which selects the lines that
match it, or by two addresses separated by a comma, which select a range
of lines.
Without an address, a command applies to every line.
An address is one of:
.TP
.I n
Line number
.IR n ,
counting from 1.
The count runs across files, but restarts for each file with
.BR \-i .
A lone 0 is the same as no address.
.TP
.B $
The last line of the input, or with
.BR \-i ,
of each file.
.TP
.BI / regex /
Any line whose pattern space contains a match for
.IR regex .
.TP
\fB\e%\fIregex\fB%\fR
The same, with
.B %
or any other character in place of the slashes.
.PP
A range starts at a line selected by the first address and ends at the
first line after it selected by the second address.
If the second address is a number that is not greater than the number of
the first line, the range is that line alone.
A second address that is a regular expression is first tried on the line
after the start of the range.
The second address may also be
.BI + n \fR,
which ends the range
.I n
lines after the first, or
.BR $ .
A range whose end is never found extends to the end of the input.
After a range has ended, a new one starts at the next line selected by
the first address.
.PP
A
.B !\&
after the address or range selects the lines that it does not select.
Only one
.B !\&
is allowed.
.SS Regular expressions
Regular expressions are POSIX basic or extended regular expressions, as
implemented by the GNU regex code built into busybox, with its
extensions.
Extended expressions are used with
.B \-r
or
.BR \-E .
In a basic expression,
.BR \e+ ,
.BR \e? ,
and
.B \e|
are the repetition, optional, and alternation operators that
.BR + ,
.BR ? ,
and
.B |
are in an extended expression, and
.BR + ,
.BR ? ,
.BR | ,
.BR { ,
.BR } ,
.BR ( ,
and
.B )
match themselves.
In an extended expression, a backslash makes these characters match
themselves.
An interval is
.BI \e{ m , n \e}
in a basic expression and
.BI { m , n }
in an extended one, and a group is
.BI \e( expr \e)
or
\fB(\fIexpr\fB)\fR.
Both kinds accept back-references
.B \e1
to
.BR \e9 ,
.B \ew
for a word character, which is an ASCII letter, digit, or underscore,
.B \eW
for any other character,
.B \es
and
.B \eS
for whitespace and anything else,
.B \eb
and
.B \eB
for a word boundary and its absence,
.B \e<
and
.B \e>
for the start and end of a word, and
.B \e\(ga
and
.B \e\(aq
for the start and end of the pattern space.
.PP
Before a regular expression is compiled,
.B sed
converts
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
and
.B \er
to LF, tab, and CR, including inside a bracket expression.
In an
.B s
command it also replaces a backslash and the delimiter by the bare
delimiter, so in
.B s|a\e|b|X|
the expression is
.BR a|b ,
which matches those three characters in a basic expression and is an
alternation in an extended one.
In an address the backslash stays, and the regular expression compiler
sees it.
A delimiter inside a bracket expression does not end the regular
expression.
.PP
The pattern space can hold several lines joined by LF.
A
.B .\&
or a bracket expression that excludes characters also matches an LF,
and
.B ^
and
.B $
match only at the start and end of the pattern space, not at an LF.
.PP
Matching works on bytes.
A
.B .\&
or a bracket expression matches one byte, not one non-ASCII character,
and character classes such as
.B [:alpha:]
and the case folding of the
.B I
flag cover only ASCII letters.
.PP
An empty regular expression, as in
.B //
or
.BR s//x/ ,
stands for the last regular expression used.
For
.B s
that is the last one used when the command runs, and for an address, the
last address regular expression before it in the script, ignoring those
of
.B s
commands.
.PP
With the
.B g
flag or a number
.IR N ,
each match after the first is searched for in the rest of the pattern
space as if it began there, though not at the start of a line.
So
.BR \e\(ga ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \eB ,
and
.B \e<
can match differently there:
.B s/\e<a/X/g
changes
.B aaa
to
.BR XXX .
.SS Commands
Each command may have the addresses described above, except as noted.
.TP
.B =
Write the current line number and an LF to the output.
.TP
.B {
Start a block: the commands up to the matching
.B }
run only for the selected lines.
Blocks nest.
A
.B {
with no matching
.B }
is an error only when its block is skipped.
.TP
.B }
End a block.
A
.B }
with no matching
.B {
does nothing.
.TP
\fB:\fIlabel\fR
Define a label, the word after the colon with leading blanks skipped.
It does nothing when reached.
If a label is defined twice, the first one is used.
.TP
\fBa\fR \fItext\fR
Queue
.I text
to be written after the current line, at the end of the cycle or when
.B n
or
.B N
reads the next line.
Takes at most one address.
.IP
The text may follow the command on the same line, or start on the next
line after a backslash at the end of the line.
Blanks after the command letter are skipped.
If they are followed by a backslash or a newline, that is removed too,
and any blanks after it are kept.
The text continues over lines that end in a backslash, which is removed
and the newline kept.
It ends at the end of the line, so semicolons and braces are part of it.
In the text,
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
and
.B \er
stand for LF, tab, and CR, and a backslash before any other character is
removed.
The text is written followed by an LF.
.TP
\fBb\fR [\fIlabel\fR]
Branch to
.IR label ,
or to the end of the script when there is none, where the pattern space
is written as usual.
A label that is not defined is an error only when the branch is run.
.TP
\fBc\fR \fItext\fR
Delete the pattern space and write
.IR text ,
which has the form described under
.BR a ,
then start the next cycle.
With a range, the text is written once, at the end of the range, and
nothing is written if the input ends first.
Otherwise it is written for every selected line.
.TP
.B d
Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle, without writing
anything.
.TP
.B D
If the pattern space contains no LF, act like
.BR d .
Otherwise delete the text up to and including the first LF and run the
script again on what is left, without writing the pattern space and
without reading a new line.
.TP
.B g
Replace the pattern space with the hold space.
.TP
.B G
Append an LF and the hold space to the pattern space.
.TP
.B h
Replace the hold space with the pattern space.
.TP
.B H
Append an LF and the pattern space to the hold space.
.TP
\fBi\fR \fItext\fR
Write
.I text
now, before the current line.
The text has the form described under
.BR a .
Takes at most one address.
.TP
.B n
Write the pattern space unless
.B \-n
is given, then replace it with the next input line.
If there is no next line, the input ends there, without running the
rest of the script.
.TP
.B N
Append an LF and the next input line to the pattern space.
If there is no next line,
.B sed
writes the pattern space unless
.B \-n
is given, and the input ends there, without running the rest of the
script.
.TP
.B p
Write the pattern space and an LF.
.TP
.B P
Write the pattern space up to the first LF, or all of it if there is
none, and an LF.
.TP
.B q
Write the pattern space unless
.B \-n
is given, write any queued text, and end the input without reading more
of it.
There is no exit code argument.
With
.B \-i
it ends only the current file, and with a
.B \-
operand only the current part of the input, as described in
.BR NOTES .
.TP
\fBr\fR \fIfile\fR
Queue the contents of
.I file
to be written after the current line, like
.BR a .
The file name is the rest of the line, with leading blanks skipped.
A file that cannot be opened is silently ignored.
Each line of the file is written followed by an LF, so a missing final
LF is added and a CR before an LF is removed.
Takes at most one address.
.TP
\fBs/\fIregex\fB/\fIreplacement\fB/\fR[\fIflags\fR]
Replace the first match of
.I regex
in the pattern space with
.IR replacement .
The slashes may be any other character, and then a backslash before that
character in the regular expression or the replacement stands for the
character itself.
.IP
In the replacement,
.B &
and
.B \e0
stand for the matched text, and
.B \e1
to
.B \e9
for the text matched by that parenthesized subexpression, or for
nothing if it did not match.
Also
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
and
.B \er
stand for LF, tab, and CR, and a backslash followed by a newline stands
for a newline.
A backslash before any other character stands for that character, so
.B \e&
is a literal ampersand and
.B \e\e
a backslash.
.IP
The flags can be combined.
Blanks between them are skipped, and they end at a newline, semicolon,
.BR } ,
or comment.
.RS
.TP
.B g
Replace every match.
.TP
.I N
Replace only the
.IR N th
match.
A number of 0 is the same as
.BR g .
It is ignored if the regular expression starts with
.BR ^ .
If both
.B g
and a number are given, the later one counts.
.TP
.B p
If a replacement was made, write the pattern space.
.TP
.BR i ", " I
Ignore case.
.TP
.BI w " file"
If a replacement was made, write the pattern space to
.IR file ,
as the
.B w
command does.
This flag takes the rest of the line.
.RE
.TP
\fBt\fR [\fIlabel\fR]
Branch like
.B b
if an
.B s
command has replaced something since the start of the cycle or the last
.B n
or
.BR t .
The next
.B t
or
.B T
then sees no replacement.
.TP
\fBT\fR [\fIlabel\fR]
Branch like
.B b
if no
.B s
command has replaced anything since the start of the cycle or the last
.B n
or
.BR t .
.TP
\fBw\fR \fIfile\fR
Write the pattern space and its terminator to
.IR file .
The file name is the rest of the line, with leading blanks skipped.
.B sed
creates or truncates the file when it reads the script, before any input,
even if the command never runs.
Commands that name the same file share it.
The names
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/null
work.
.I /dev/stdout
is a second stream on standard output with a buffer of its own, so what
is written to it can come out of order with the other output.
.TP
.B x
Exchange the pattern space and the hold space.
.TP
\fBy/\fIsource\fB/\fIdest\fB/\fR
Replace each byte of
.I source
in the pattern space by the corresponding byte of
.IR dest .
The strings are bytes, not characters.
If they differ in length, the extra bytes of the longer one are ignored,
and if
.I source
repeats a byte, the first occurrence counts.
In both strings,
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \er ,
and a backslash and the delimiter are escapes, but any other backslash
is an ordinary byte, so
.B \e\e
is two backslashes.
A
.B [
in
.I source
starts a bracket expression, as in a regular expression, and a delimiter
inside it does not end
.IR source ,
so
.B y/[/x/
is an error.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-nr ,
and may follow operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-fscript.sed ,
or be the next argument, except for the optional suffix of
.BR \-i ,
which must be attached.
Long options may be abbreviated to any unique prefix.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR \fIscript\fR, \fB\-\-expression\fR=\fIscript\fR
Add
.I script
to the commands to run.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIscriptfile\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR=\fIscriptfile\fR
Add the commands in
.I scriptfile
to the commands to run.
Lines may end with CR LF.
A
.I scriptfile
of
.B \-
is standard input.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR[\fIsuffix\fR], \fB\-\-in\-place\fR[=\fIsuffix\fR]
Edit the files in place instead of writing to standard output.
Each
.I file
is replaced by the output for it, which is written to a temporary file
in the same directory first.
There must be at least one
.IR file ,
and a
.I file
named
.B \-
is not standard input.
.IP
With a
.IR suffix ,
the original is first renamed to the file name followed by
.IR suffix ,
replacing any such file.
The suffix is appended as given: a
.B *
in it is not replaced by the file name, and a slash in it does not
select a directory.
Because
.B \-i
takes the rest of its argument, in
.B \-in
the
.B n
is the suffix.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR, \fB\-\-silent\fR
Do not write the pattern space at the end of the cycle.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-E\fR, \fB\-\-regexp\-extended\fR
Use extended regular expressions.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-binary\fR
Keep the CR of a CR LF line ending as part of the line, so that regular
expressions see it, and write it back.
Without it the CR is removed, so CRLF text is written with LF line
endings.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and later arguments are
ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.B This is not GNU sed version 4.0
and exit with status 0, so that configure scripts accept this
.BR sed .
It is recognized only as the first argument, and later arguments are
ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first non-option argument ends the options, so that later
arguments such as
.B \-n
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime replaces each argument containing wildcards with the names
of the files it matches, if any, even when the argument is quoted.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for its child processes, so commands run from the busybox shell, which
expands wildcards itself, receive their arguments as given.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_UMASK
for the files that
.B w
and
.B \-i
create, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, an invalid script or regular expression, an error found
only when a command runs, such as a branch to an undefined label, a
script file, input file, or
.B w
file that could not be opened, or an
.B \-i
file that could not be replaced.
Input files that cannot be opened are skipped, and the others are still
processed.
.TP
.B 4
Standard output or another output file could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error, beginning with
.BR sed: .
A usage error also prints the usage message.
.SH EXAMPLES
Replace every
.B foo
with
.BR bar :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \(aqs/foo/bar/g\(aq in.txt >out.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Edit source files in place, keeping each original with a
.B .bak
suffix:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \-i.bak \(aqs/colour/color/g\(aq *.c *.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print lines 10 through 20:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \-n \(aq10,20p\(aq log.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the first 10 lines and stop reading:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed 10q log.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove trailing blanks and delete empty lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \-e \(aqs/[[:space:]]*$//\(aq \-e \(aq/^$/d\(aq notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Delete everything from a line holding
.B BEGIN
through the next line holding
.BR END :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \(aq/^BEGIN$/,/^END$/d\(aq data.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Insert a line before each match:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \(aq/^int main/i /* entry point */\(aq main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reverse the order of the lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \-n \(aq1!G;h;$p\(aq file.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Change the backslashes in a list of Windows paths to slashes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \(aqs/\e\e/\e//g\(aq paths.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert CRLF line endings to LF, then LF line endings to CRLF:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \(aq\(aq dos.txt >unix.txt
$ sed \(aqs/$/\er/\(aq unix.txt >dos.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Edit a file in place without changing its CRLF line endings:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sed \-b \-i \(aqs/foo/bar/\(aq win.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR sed :
.IP \(bu 2
GNU options such as
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-\-posix ,
and
.B \-\-debug
are invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
The commands
.BR e ,
.BR F ,
.BR Q ,
.BR R ,
.BR v ,
.BR W ,
and
.B z
are reported as unsupported commands.
The command
.B l
is accepted and does nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
An address cannot be
.IB first ~ step\fR,
nor can a range end in
.BI ~ n\fR.
A range that starts at line 0, as in
.BR 0,/regex/ ,
never matches.
There are no
.B I
and
.B M
modifiers after an address.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B s
command has no
.BR m ,
.BR M ,
or
.B e
flag, and no case conversion: in a replacement,
.BR \eU ,
.BR \eL ,
.BR \eu ,
.BR \el ,
and
.B \eE
stand for the letters.
Escapes such as
.B \ex41
and
.B \ea
are not recognized either, in replacements and in the text of
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
and
.BR c .
.IP \(bu 2
The commands
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
and
.B r
accept only one address, and
.B q
takes no exit code.
.IP \(bu 2
A comment that starts with
.B n
turns on
.BR \-n ,
and every
.B \-e
script is read before any
.B \-f
file.
.IP \(bu 2
In
.BR s ,
a number and
.B g
do not combine:
.B 2g
replaces every match, and
.B g2
only the second.
.IP \(bu 2
When the strings of
.B y
differ in length, the extra bytes are ignored, not an error.
.PP
Standard input and output are binary.
Because
.B sed
removes the CR of every CR LF line ending it reads, and writes LF alone,
it converts a CRLF file to an LF file, with
.B \-i
too, unless
.B \-b
is given.
To write CRLF line endings, add the CR with
.B s/$/\er/
or pass the output through
.BR unix2dos (1).
The text of
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
and
.BR c ,
and the replacement of
.BR s ,
treat a backslash as an escape, so write each backslash of a Windows
path in them twice.
File names after
.B r
and
.B w
and in
.B s///w
are taken as they are.
.PP
With
.BR \-i ,
the new file is created afresh and renamed over the old one, so it does
not inherit the old file's attributes, such as read-only, and is
created read-only when the mask in
.B BB_UMASK
has the owner write bit.
The hold space is kept from one file to the next.
A
.B q
ends only the current file, and the next file is still processed.
Windows cannot replace a file that is still open, so a
.B q
that runs on a line other than the last of a file makes the replacement
fail.
.B sed
reports
.BR "can't move" ,
exits with status 1, and leaves the file unchanged.
For example,
.B sed \-i 2q
fails on a file with more than 2 lines.
.PP
Without
.BR \-i ,
each
.B \-
operand other than the last ends a part of the input, and the parts are
processed one after another, each as if it were all the input.
Line numbers start at 1 again in each part, although a line number
address that has already matched does not match again, and
.B $
is the last line of the part.
A
.B q
ends only its part, and the next part first reads what that part left
unread, except for one line, which is lost.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The scripts given on the command line are in the same encoding, and
script files are read as bytes.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/tty
(the console) work as on Unix, and
.I /dev/zero
and
.I /dev/urandom
can be read but not written.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
When standard output is a console, bytes above 0x7F are converted for
display from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version
1903 and later, to the console's code page, and escape sequences in the
output take effect.
